Manual review pass: fixes several converter/prose defects.

- Normalizes inconsistent comma spacing before conjoined equations in item
  conditions (e.g. "y(t)=t, 0\le \text{t}\le1" -> "y(t)=t,\,0\le t\le1"; the
  spurious \text{t} is also dropped so the variable renders in the same math
  font as every other occurrence of t).
- Splits three cases where a plain-English "to"/"from...to" connecting two
  separate mathematical expressions had been fused into one math span via
  \text{to} with manual \, spacing (items on x=0..x=5, y=x^3 from (0,0) to
  (2,8), and A(1,0,0) to B(2,0,0)), matching the checklist's "keep English
  outside math mode" rule used elsewhere in this collection.
- Drops a stray \text{C} that rendered the curve label upright, inconsistent
  with the plain italic C{:} label used by every other item in this file.
- Restores the missing $\int_{C}^{}\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}$ line-integral
  notation in the "Evaluate F(x,y,z)=..." item -- as written it asked to
  "evaluate" a vector field, which is not a well-formed instruction; the
  parallel item later in the same section ("Evaluate \int_C F.dr, where
  F(x,y,z)=... and C: ...") makes the intended statement unambiguous.
- Rewrites the flux item's "Calculate flux F orientated counterclockwise..."
  into "Calculate the flux of $\mathbf{F}$, oriented counterclockwise, ...":
  fixes a bare (non-math) F, the non-US spelling "orientated", and phrasing
  inconsistent with the other flux items in the same block.
- In the inverse-square force-field item: promotes the two bare prose "F"
  references to $\mathbf{F}$, promotes bare "k" to $k$ (both already
  consistent with the file's own conventions), and changes the constant's
  numerator from $\mathbf{k}$ (bold, i.e. typeset identically to the k basis
  vector used throughout this file) to plain italic $k$, since the same
  sentence identifies it as a scalar constant, not a vector.
- Wraps bare "x-axis"/"xy-plane" prose in math mode ($x$-axis, $xy$-plane) in
  the four places they occur bare in this file, matching this collection's
  established $x$-/$y$-axis notation.

No blank or whitespace-only lines were added, removed, or altered.

A later pass found one exercise where a range condition was jammed directly
against its variable with no separator at all -- "$z=t(0\le t\le T)$" reads
as $t$ multiplied by the parenthesized range, not "$z=t$, where $0\le t\le
T$" -- unlike the file's own convention for attaching a range condition
(comma, thin space, no parens; e.g. "$...+t\mathbf{k},\,0\le t\le2\pi$").
Changed to "$z=t,\,0\le t\le T$" to match.

